00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:03.270 align:start position:0%
 
hi  this is dr gregory sadler i'm a  professor of philosophy  and the president and founder of an  educational consulting company called  reason i o  where we put philosophy into practice  i've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years  and i find that many people run into  difficulties reading classic  philosophical texts  sometimes it's the way things are said  or how the text is structured  but the concepts themselves are not  always that complicated and that's where  i come in  to help students and lifelong learners  i've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube  many viewers say they find them useful  what you're currently watching is part  of a new series of shorter videos  each of them focused on one core concept  from an important philosophical text  i hope you find it useful as well  in the meno during the discussion  between  socrates and this foreigner mino who  claims to be able to talk all about  virtue and tell  socrates what virtue in fact is  there comes a point in the dialogue a  very important  turning point where meno winds up in  perplexity they've tried to define  virtue several times socrates has  pointed out some objections with mino's  attempted  definitions of virtue and mino now finds  himself  at a loss and he says socrates this is  why  people say that you are like  a stingray you're like  an animal which stuns it's  it's prey or stuns the the things that  it comes into contact  with and i am similarly  at a loss as if i'd been hit by that  sort of thing  you know we often say something hit me  like a ton of bricks  it's the same sort of idea that you know  for a moment at least we are  unable to come to terms with whatever is  going on we're  we're at a loss we can't make sense of  it we thought that we knew what was  going on  and now we find that we're not able to  to give a  coherent account that we thought we were  able to do before so what we become  aware of  is an inability and we feel it as a loss  because we think that we had some sort  of capacity  and then somebody else took it away from  us here's where it gets particularly  interesting  this analogy or this likening as  socrates calls it  has socrates being like a person who  stings  other people who have knowledge and he  thereby takes it away from them  and socrates says no what i actually  communicate to you  and is my own perplexity i don't know  myself and so i'm able to get across to  you  the fact that i don't know and the fact  that you  trying to inform me with what you claim  to know didn't really work and you don't  know either  so it's a way of socrates communicating  a state of  necessity a state of awareness of one's  own  lack of knowledge one's own ability  to produce some sort of coherent  systematic  covers it all account of whatever the  phenomenon is  and so socrates says look i i myself  don't have  knowledge um this is revealing to you  your own lack of knowledge  and the question now that we have to ask  is  is this a bad thing or a good thing and  that really depends  on your perspective and your point of  view  if you view being able to talk  whether you actually know what you're  talking about or not about some  phenomenon  as the good thing and somebody else  comes along and shows you up and says  well you really you  you can't answer questions you can't  coherently argue for whatever it is that  you're advancing  you really don't know what you're  talking about somebody comes along and  does that  then you feel that they've taken  something away from you it's a bad thing  but if you're actually ignorant here's  another way to look at it this is the  way that socrates looks at it and the  way that mino i think looks at it  if you are actually ignorant then  isn't it advance to find out that you  are indeed ignorant if you are  resourceless  resourceless isn't it an advance  to find out that you are lacking the  resources that you thought that you have  you can only go out and acquire  knowledge if you don't already think  that you know it  you can only go out and get the  resources that you need  if indeed you realize that you don't  have them if you think that you have  them already you'll never go to get them  you know we could put this in terms of  other things you won't start  exercising and getting your body  stronger  until you realize that you you actually  have a need for it if you feel that  you're already strong enough well then  you don't need to do anything additional  and then when it comes time to actually  use your body  or use your resources or use your  knowledge  you find out that you you can't actually  do it that you don't measure up that  what you thought you had  you're actually missing so socrates is  actually doing a very good  thing for me now and and all the other  interlocutors by  extension who he is communicating with  who is showing look you you say  this but you actually are wrong about  that you  you say that you know that x is is y  but it turns out that x isn't y you  say that you have some sort of capacity  or skill but  when you're actually put to the test you  don't you don't have that now you can go  out and actually  do that so you could think of this as  sort of a  you know a harsh medicine rather than  just you know an attack  a harsh medicine that takes away from  the person  something that they thought that they  had but actually just reveals  that they never had it in the first  place in this case  it has to do with knowledge but we could  think of this in terms of  of other things as well and whether one  person is better off  knowing that they're in that case one  last thing i do want to point out  oftentimes uh people will say well if  you yourself don't measure up to this  standard you can't hold  other people to this this standard and  so  you know in moral terms people will  often say look if if you're not a moral  person yourself you don't get to  criticize other people's morality  or their choices or their their bad  habits or anything like that  and is that something that could be  objected to  this socrates  is willing to admit that he himself does  not  know that he himself is perplexed  that's what he says he communicates to  the  other person he gets it across to them  that not only is socrates perplexed and  socrates is a pretty smart guy  but that person is also perplexed they  don't know what what they're actually  what they think they know what they're  talking about what they perhaps are an  expert in  does socrates have to possess some sort  of  knowledge in order to be able to do this  the answer is no we can in fact realize  our  own lack of knowledge our own ignorance  about things  provided that we don't suffer from other  intellectual difficulties like  stubbornness  or foolishness or obstinacy or  things along those lines pride so  socrates is is actually  doing something here that he like i said  he's going to do it many other dialogues  this is part of the socratic method this  is a  really key point in the dialogue where  meno  is getting it it's painful for him to  get it  it feels bad not just in a sense of  perhaps getting a headache  or embarrassment but it really  bothers him that he thought that he knew  what he was talking about and turns out  that he's not able to  to make sense of it with another person  this is  a key part of socratic  inquiry and it prepares us for being  able to carry on  further inquiry we clear away the ground  of what we  thought we knew but don't actually know  so that we can now  build up something that we actually can  say we know

